# What NOT to upload to the EAS build server.
#
# THIS FILE MUST LIVE AT THE GIT ROOT. EAS archives from the repository root,
# not from the Expo project directory, so an .easignore inside apps/mobile/ is
# never read. Mine was there for two builds: the archive stayed 1.1 GB, and the
# 987 MB of source PDFs sitting inside the project root went to the build
# worker for Metro to crawl.
#
# .easignore REPLACES .gitignore for uploads rather than adding to it, so
# everything that must stay out has to be repeated here.

# ── the corpus: 987 MB of source PDFs, used by no build step ─────────────────
# ANCHORED WITH A LEADING SLASH, and that is the whole point.
#
# `content/` unanchored matches a directory named `content` at ANY depth, so it
# also excluded apps/mobile/assets/content/studymate.db — the question bank.
# The upload then had no database, Metro could not resolve the require() in
# src/db/content.ts, and the build died in the "Bundle JavaScript" phase with
# no message beyond "Unknown error".
#
# A leading slash pins each pattern to the repository root, which is what was
# meant: exclude /content, keep apps/mobile/assets/content.
/content/
/dist/
/apps/api/
/tools/

# ── build output, caches, native projects EAS regenerates ────────────────────
node_modules/
**/node_modules/
apps/mobile/android/
apps/mobile/ios/
apps/mobile/.expo/
.expo/
__pycache__/
**/__pycache__/
*.pyc

# ── local noise ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
*.log
*.tmp
.DS_Store
.git/
.claude/
.claude-flow/
.octogent/
.vscode/
.idea/

# ── secrets, belt and braces ────────────────────────────────────────────────
.env
*.env
!.env.example
*.keystore
*.jks
